  "query_preview": "..\nDry and warm conditions across the region today through\nThursday. The upper level ridge will warm the region a few\ndegrees above normal before a change in pattern. \n\nEnsemble models show a series of weather systems that will pass\nthrough starting on Friday. Our first system: A trough will push\ninto the western states with a closed low into the Pacific\nNorthwest and another cutoff low on the southern tip of the\ntrough by Baja. The southern system will travel south of\nArizona, bringing 30-50% chance for rain showers across\nSoutheast Arizona on Friday to early Saturday. The International\nBorder will see the bulk of the showers on Friday and then\nshifting to the northern areas late Friday to Saturday as the \nlow transitions out of the region. Forecasted rain totals will \nbe on the lighter end, a tenth to quarter of an inch. There can\nbe pockets of half to three quarters of inch from any\nthunderstorm. Sunday appears to be the calmer day before the \nnext system coming in on Monday. \n\nThe second system will swing through Southeast Arizona starting\nMonday and exiting by Tuesday to early Wednesday. There are\ndifferences among the models on the timing and evolution of the\nsystem, including the difference on rainfall totals. For \nexample, the European ensemble members lean wetter than the \nother two ensemble model members. Yet, PWATS will be above the \n90th percentile through the event but it will depend on the \nposition and timing of the trough. Overall, rainfall totals will\nbe higher than the first system. 24 hour rainfall probabilities\nfor half inch or higher are 30-45% across southeast Arizona \nwith higher probabilities (50-60%) in the mountains and north of\nTucson. This system will also bring cooler temperatures and breezes\nfor the first half of the week.\n\nLooking far ahead into the late next week, ensembles show \nthe pattern continuing to be be dynamic and troughy.",
